8. Determine whether any police incident number, report, arrest record or other official record exists.
9. Identify the original report concerning the woman and the encounter.
10. Obtain the original police or agency record concerning the reported consensual nature of the encounter, if it exists and can lawfully be disclosed.
11. Investigate the allegation that a woman was paid to discredit me by examining relevant original evidence.
12. Identify the alleged NDA and seek the original document or any surviving copy.
13. Establish who required Ben to sign it and under what authority.
14. Determine whether Benâ€™s account of the document disappearing from his device can be independently corroborated.
15. Give Ben an appropriate opportunity to confirm, correct or deny the statements attributed to him, without pressure or intimidation.
16. Record any limitations arising from unavailable records, privacy restrictions, confidentiality obligations or the passage of time.

25. Protection against prejudicial use of untested material

The Ben correspondence should not be used selectively to discredit me while ignoring its complete context.

Nor should the correspondence be presented as a proven police or intelligence finding when it is presently a set of statements attributed to a private correspondent.

I request that the same evidentiary standards be applied to all parties.

If an allegation concerning me was based on a report, the original report and its provenance should be disclosed to the extent legally permissible. If the report was influenced by inaccurate, fabricated or paid-for information, that possibility should be investigated through the underlying evidence.

I also request that no person be prejudged or publicly accused on the basis of an unverified interpretation of these messages.

26. Final finding sought

The appropriate finding at this stage is that the Ben-NDIS-Help and Ben-Ndis-Help-large correspondence contains serious statements warranting independent authentication and investigation.

The messages are potentially important evidence of what Ben communicated to me, including his reported account of a police-described â€œclose callâ€, an alleged payment to discredit me, and an NDA he said he was required to sign.

The underlying events remain matters for independent verification.

I request that these messages be treated as a distinct and important evidentiary stream within the broader safeguarding and institutional accountability investigation, rather than being omitted, minimised or treated as established fact without examination.

The saved archive material supporting this addition includes The Witness Remains and The Sovereign Witness.

The key distinction: Benâ€™s messages, if authenticated, are evidence of what he told you. The alleged assassination attempt, the alleged payment to the woman, the police assessment and the NDAâ€™s origin and legal status still require the underlying records. That distinction strengthens the request for an investigation rather than weakening it.


EVIDENCE ANALYSIS â€” SCOTT TREADWELLâ€™S THREE-POINT OBSERVATION

Federal Court Public Interest Disclosure Response â€” 27 March 2023

Author: Scott Treadwell, General Counsel and authorised Public Interest Disclosure officer
Subject: Dr Richard William McLean
Relevant disclosure: Public interest disclosure dated 7 March 2023
Document date: 27 March 2023

POINT 1 â€” PERVERTING THE COURSE OF JUSTICE

The first category identified was conduct that:

â€œperverts, or is engaged in the purpose of perverting, or attempting to pervert, the course of justiceâ€.

Significance

This is important because it identifies the administration of justice as one of the categories of wrongdoing you had raised in your disclosure.

It establishes that your concerns included the possibility of interference with lawful processes, the integrity of evidence, the handling of allegations or other conduct potentially affecting the proper administration of justice.

The significance is not that Treadwell established that justice had been perverted. Rather, his written response records that this was one of the statutory categories relevant to the concerns you had presented.

An independent investigator should compare the original disclosure with the subsequent handling of your complaints, evidence, employment status and legal proceedings to determine whether any identifiable conduct satisfies the relevant legal elements.

POINT 2 â€” MALADMINISTRATION

The second category was:

â€œconduct that constitutes maladministrationâ€.

Significance

This is important because it places your concerns within the framework of administrative accountability.

Maladministration can involve improper, unreasonable, negligent or otherwise defective administrative conduct, depending on the applicable legal framework and facts.

The significance is that your disclosure was not confined to a personal disagreement or an isolated complaint. It raised concerns about the conduct and administration of public functions.

The response provides a documentary starting point for examining whether decisions, omissions, procedural handling, institutional correspondence or administrative classifications were lawful, reasonable, consistent and properly documented.

The relevant records should be compared against the reasons given by each institution, the evidence available at the time and any subsequent contradictory decisions.

POINT 3 â€” DANGER TO HEALTH OR SAFETY

The third category concerned conduct that:

â€œunreasonably results in, or increases, danger or risk of danger to the health or safety of one or more personsâ€.

Significance

This is particularly relevant to the safeguarding dimension of the present notice.

It demonstrates that your original disclosure included concerns about conduct potentially creating or increasing risks to health and safety.

That category is directly relevant to the need to examine your subsequent reports of threats, accommodation safety concerns, repeated offers of unidentified drugs, alleged intimidation and the consequences of institutional decisions.

It does not prove that any particular person endangered you. It does, however, provide a documentary basis for asking whether the safety concerns you raised were properly assessed, referred, recorded and addressed.

The present safeguarding investigation should compare the 2023 disclosure with later incidents and determine whether any recurring, independently verifiable risk indicators were overlooked or inadequately addressed.

â¸»

OVERALL SIGNIFICANCE OF THE THREE-POINT OBSERVATION

The three categories are significant because they cover three distinct dimensions of public accountability:

1. Integrity of justice: whether conduct interfered with, or attempted to interfere with, the proper administration of justice.
2. Integrity of administration: whether public administration was conducted properly and lawfully.
3. Protection of people: whether conduct created or increased danger to health or safety.

Together, these categories demonstrate the breadth of the concerns you placed before the authorised disclosure officer in March 2023.

The response should therefore be retained as an important primary documentary record in the chronology of your complaints.

It must not be misrepresented as a finding that the three forms of wrongdoing occurred. Its evidentiary value lies in recording the nature of the concerns you had formally raised, the statutory categories considered and the subsequent administrative treatment of your disclosure.

THE EMPLOYMENT-STATUS OBSERVATION

Treadwell also wrote:

â€œOn the information you have provided me, I am satisfied that you are, or were, an employee with the Department of Social Servicesâ€¦â€

This is important because it records his acceptance, on the information supplied, of your employment connection with the Department of Social Services.

That observation should be compared with later employment-status classifications, including any treatment of you as a contractor or sole trader by other institutions.

It does not, by itself, resolve every legal question about your employment status, but it is a relevant contemporaneous statement by an authorised officer.

THE REASONABLE-GROUNDS OBSERVATION

Treadwell also stated:

â€œIt is difficult to determine whether this belief is based on reasonable grounds or not.â€

This wording is significant because it records uncertainty about whether the belief underlying your disclosure was reasonably grounded.

It should not be represented as a finding that your concerns were false. Nor should it be represented as confirmation that they were true.

The original disclosure and supporting documents must be examined to establish what evidence was available to the officer and whether the assessment adequately considered it.

THE DECISION NOT TO ALLOCATE THE DISCLOSURE

The response reportedly concluded that Treadwell was not satisfied that the disclosure had been made by a public official or authorised recipient and that no further PID action would occur.

This is an important distinction: the response acknowledged the subject matter and statutory categories but did not proceed with the disclosure under the PID process.

The investigation should examine the legal and factual basis for that decision, including the employment-status observation and the reasoning used to determine whether the disclosure qualified for further action.

RECUSAL OR STEPPING ASIDE

If the original correspondence contains a statement that Treadwell recused himself, stepped aside or declined further involvement, that exact wording must be quoted and its context established.

The presently available account establishes that he declined allocation and said no further PID action would occur. It does not establish that he recused himself specifically to protect you. Any claimed protective motive must be supported by the original correspondence.

REQUIRED INVESTIGATIVE ACTION

The original 27 March 2023 letter and the 7 March 2023 disclosure should be preserved together.

An independent reviewer should establish:

* What evidence accompanied the original disclosure.
* Which three statutory categories were raised.
* Why the disclosure was not allocated for further PID action.
* How the employment-status observation was reconciled with the decision.
* Whether the reasonable-grounds issue was properly assessed.
* Whether subsequent institutions received or considered the original disclosure.
* Whether later decisions contradicted or overlooked relevant information.
* Whether any specific safety risks were subsequently reported and addressed.

Conclusion: The 27 March 2023 response is a significant documentary milestone because it records that concerns concerning the administration of justice, maladministration and danger to health or safety had already been formally raised. It should be cross-referenced against subsequent institutional records rather than treated as an isolated correspondence item.


I have combined the material into one chronological section, retaining the individual dates, the distinction between the two Prime Ministerial communications, the Mark Dreyfus referral, the Ombudsman decisions, your concerns about IGIS and Steve Iasonidis, the evidentiary significance, and the formal requests for investigation. I have removed the duplicated passages while preserving their substance and the source links.
PRIME MINISTERIAL ESCALATION, MARK DREYFUS REFERRAL, COMMONWEALTH OMBUDSMAN, IGIS AND THE EFFECTIVENESS OF GOVERNMENT OVERSIGHT
Subject: Dr Richard William McLean â€” Barran Dodgerâ€¨Relevant period: 31 May 2022 â€“ 20 January 2026â€¨Jurisdiction: Commonwealth of Australiaâ€¨Purpose: Documentary chronology, evidentiary analysis and formal request for independent investigation.
1. Overview â€” the significance of the documentary sequence
The documentary record identifies an important sequence of attempts to obtain government-level consideration of serious matters through the Office of the Prime Minister, the Attorney-Generalâ€™s Department, the Commonwealth Ombudsman and the Inspector-General of Intelligence and Security (IGIS).
The sequence includes:
1. My letter to the Prime Minister dated 31 May 2022.
2. The Prime Ministerâ€™s Office response dated 12 December 2022, stating that my email had not been forwarded.
3. A subsequent email to the Prime Minister dated 5 July 2023, raising further matters, including concerns relating to ASIO.
4. The Attorney-Generalâ€™s Department response dated 19 September 2023, reference MC23-028244, responding on behalf of Attorney-General Mark Dreyfus.
5. The Departmentâ€™s direction to IGIS and the Commonwealth Ombudsman.
6. The Commonwealth Ombudsmanâ€™s earlier decision dated 18 June 2023 not to allocate the relevant public interest disclosure.
7. The later Commonwealth Ombudsman service-restriction correspondence dated 8 August 2025.
8. Further records identified in my archive concerning ASIO and IGIS, including records dated 2 April 2025 and 20 January 2026.
9. My concerns concerning my former fiancÃ©, Steve Iasonidis, whom I identify as having an association with ASIO, and my belief that my complaints concerning him were not adequately investigated.
The significance is not simply that government correspondence was redirected. It is that the effectiveness of those referrals must be examined against the earlier handling of my complaints, the Ombudsmanâ€™s recorded non-allocation decision, the later restriction on my access to the Ombudsman and the particular concerns I had raised about intelligence-agency oversight.
The central question is whether the government provided an effective, accessible and independent mechanism for examining the substance of my complaints, or whether the correspondence resulted in successive administrative referrals without a demonstrable substantive investigation or protective remedy.
This chronology should be considered alongside the wider documentary archive and the history of my attempts to obtain accountability, protection and access to justice.
2. Original letter to the Prime Minister â€” 31 May 2022
Primary source: 31 May 2022 â€” Letter to the Prime Ministerâ 
On 31 May 2022, I wrote to the Prime Minister seeking consideration of serious matters documented in my correspondence and supporting records.
This communication represented an attempt to bring my concerns to the highest level of the Australian Government and to obtain government-level consideration of matters that I believed required intervention and independent examination.
The original letter is an essential primary document. Its precise contents must be considered, including the allegations, named institutions, evidence, requested remedies and any specific action I asked the Prime Minister to take.
Evidentiary significance
The letter establishes the date on which I sought Prime Ministerial consideration, subject to authentication of the original document and transmission record.
It is relevant to establishing:
* What concerns I had formally communicated by May 2022.
* Whether I identified particular Commonwealth agencies or individuals.
* Whether I requested an investigation, protection, ministerial intervention or another remedy.
* Whether I supplied supporting documents or references to previous complaints.
* Whether the Prime Ministerâ€™s Office had sufficient information to identify an appropriate referral pathway.
* Whether the matters were treated as a substantive complaint or merely as general correspondence.
The letter does not, by itself, establish that the Prime Minister personally read it or that the allegations were established. Its importance is that it records an attempt to obtain government-level consideration and provides the starting point for examining what happened next.
3. Prime Ministerâ€™s Office response â€” 12 December 2022
Primary source: 12 December 2022 â€” Prime Ministerâ€™s Office response: email not forwardedâ 
The subsequent Prime Ministerâ€™s Office communication, dated 12 December 2022, contains the statement:
â€œYour email has not been forwarded.â€
I regard this statement as significant because it records that my correspondence was not forwarded.
The response does not, on its face, explain whether the substance of my letter was independently assessed, whether an alternative referral was made or what effective avenue remained available for obtaining a substantive response.
Why this matters
There is an important distinction between:
1. Receiving correspondence.
2. Acknowledging correspondence.
3. Assessing the substance of the concerns.
4. Referring the matters to an appropriate minister or agency.
5. Investigating the allegations.
6. Providing findings, reasons or a protective remedy.
The recorded statement addresses forwarding. It does not establish which of the other steps occurred.
The outstanding questions are:
* Who received and assessed the original communication?
* Who decided that it would not be forwarded?
* Why was it not forwarded?
* Was the substance of the letter considered by another officer or department?
* Was it referred through another administrative or investigative pathway?
* Was the decision based on jurisdiction, policy, security considerations or another reason?
* Was an alternative mechanism for independent examination identified?
* Was I informed of any alternative mechanism or given reasons for the handling decision?
Evidentiary significance
The response is evidence of the Prime Ministerâ€™s Officeâ€™s recorded handling of my communication.
It does not establish that the Prime Minister personally rejected my allegations, that the government intended to suppress my concerns or that a coordinated decision was made to prevent investigation.
The appropriate question is whether the correspondence was substantively considered and whether the administrative handling provided a reasonable and effective response to the matters raised.
4. Subsequent Prime Ministerial email â€” 5 July 2023
The archive identifies a further email to Anthony Albanese dated 5 July 2023, which included matters concerning ASIO.
This is a separate communication from the 31 May 2022 letter. The two communications must not be merged into one event.
The distinction is important because it demonstrates a continuing attempt to obtain government-level consideration rather than a single unanswered or unforwarded communication.
The 5 July 2023 email is the correspondence subsequently referred to the Attorney-Generalâ€™s portfolio.
Evidentiary significance
The original email should be examined to establish:
* What new information was provided.
* Whether I repeated matters previously raised.
* Whether I identified particular individuals, agencies or events.
* Whether I raised concerns about ASIO-related conduct.
* Whether I raised concerns about the independence or effectiveness of existing oversight mechanisms.
* Whether I requested a ministerial decision, an investigation, protection or referral to an appropriate authority.
* Whether attachments or supporting records accompanied the email.
This document is essential to understanding the Attorney-Generalâ€™s Departmentâ€™s subsequent response. The Departmentâ€™s letter cannot be properly evaluated without comparing it with the actual communication it was answering.
5. Referral to Attorney-General Mark Dreyfus â€” 19 September 2023
Primary source: 19 September 2023 â€” Attorney-Generalâ€™s Department response, MC23-028244â 
The archive identifies the following particulars:
Particular	Recorded information
Date	19 September 2023
