The recorded response states that my 5 July 2023 email, including matters concerning ASIO, had been referred to the Attorney-General because the issues fell within his portfolio and that the Department was responding on his behalf.
The Department directed me to two oversight institutions:
* The Inspector-General of Intelligence and Security (IGIS).
* The Commonwealth Ombudsman.
Significance of the Mark Dreyfus referral
This establishes a formal ministerial correspondence pathway and identifies the Attorney-Generalâ€™s Department as the office responding on the Attorney-Generalâ€™s behalf.
It is significant because the concerns had reached a Commonwealth ministerial portfolio with responsibility for relevant national security and intelligence matters.
However, the distinction between a ministerial referral and a substantive investigation is critical.
The response does not, by itself, establish that:
* Mark Dreyfus personally investigated the allegations.
* The Attorney-General made findings concerning ASIO.
* IGIS accepted or investigated my complaint.
* The Commonwealth Ombudsman agreed to investigate.
* Any person was found to have committed misconduct.
* Any protective intervention was provided.
* The matters raised were resolved.
A referral is not a finding, an investigation or a protective intervention.
The appropriate question is what happened after the referral, whether either institution accepted responsibility for examining the allegations and whether the recommended pathways were actually capable of providing the remedy I sought.
6. Commonwealth Ombudsman â€” earlier decision not to allocate, 18 June 2023
Primary source: 18 June 2023 â€” Commonwealth Ombudsman PID decision not to allocateâ 
The chronology is particularly important because the archive identifies an earlier Commonwealth Ombudsman decision dated 18 June 2023, before the Attorney-Generalâ€™s Department response of 19 September 2023.
The document is identified as a notification of a decision not to allocate the relevant public interest disclosure.
I request that the original decision and its reasons be examined to establish precisely what disclosure it concerned, what statutory criteria were applied, what evidence was considered and why the matter was not allocated.
Why the timing matters
When the Attorney-Generalâ€™s Department directed me to the Commonwealth Ombudsman in September 2023, the Ombudsman had already issued a decision not to allocate the relevant disclosure in June 2023.
This raises a legitimate administrative question:
Why was I directed towards a pathway that had already declined to allocate the relevant disclosure, and what alternative mechanism was identified to ensure substantive consideration of the concerns?
The investigation should establish:
* Whether the June 2023 decision concerned exactly the same allegations as those raised in the July 2023 Prime Ministerial email.
* Whether the Attorney-Generalâ€™s Department knew of the earlier non-allocation decision.
* Whether the September 2023 referral was intended to initiate a new complaint or disclosure.
* Whether new evidence or a different legal basis could have been considered.
* Whether the Ombudsman had jurisdiction over the particular matters.
* Whether the Department considered the practical effectiveness of the referral.
* Whether another independent oversight pathway was available if the Ombudsman could not or would not investigate.
The Ombudsmanâ€™s statutory role
The Commonwealth Ombudsman has a statutory role in the Commonwealth public interest disclosure system.
Its functions can include investigating disclosures in circumstances where the relevant agency cannot appropriately handle the matter, or where conflicts of interest, confidentiality or reprisal concerns cannot be adequately managed.
However, not every disclosure is automatically allocated for investigation. Eligibility, jurisdiction and statutory requirements must be considered.
The existence of the Ombudsmanâ€™s statutory role does not establish that my particular disclosure qualified for investigation. Equally, the existence of an earlier non-allocation decision does not automatically establish that every possible subsequent complaint or referral was unavailable.
The original decision and the later ministerial referral must be compared.
