Similarly, the existence of an IGIS request for information does not establish that IGIS made a finding or accepted the allegations.
Why the IGIS referral matters
IGIS is an independent statutory oversight office with authority to examine the legality and propriety of intelligence-agency conduct and relevant human-rights considerations.
Its oversight functions make it an important institution for complaints concerning the conduct of intelligence agencies, including ASIO.
However, the fact that IGIS oversees ASIO does not establish that it was conflicted in my particular case. Equally, the fact that an institution is an oversight body does not remove the need to examine how it handled a particular complaint.
My concern raises specific questions about the suitability and independence of the referral:
