14. Final statement â€” the distinction between referral and remedy
The distinction is fundamental:
A referral is not an investigation.
An acknowledgement is not a finding.
A transfer of correspondence is not a protective intervention.
The naming of a statutory oversight body is not proof that it examined the complaint.
A decision not to allocate a disclosure is not necessarily a decision on the truth or falsity of the underlying allegations.
A service restriction does not, by itself, establish that every available avenue of complaint has been exhausted.
I seek an independently verifiable account of what happened to my complaints, what evidence was considered, what decisions were made, which institutions accepted responsibility and what effective remedies remain available.
I request that this chronology be considered as part of the wider documentary evidence concerning my attempts to obtain government accountability, independent oversight, protection and access to justice.
I describe the cumulative effect of unresolved referrals and procedural barriers as administrative harm and denial of access to justice. These are my analytical descriptions of the consequences I report experiencing, not findings of criminal intent by any particular officeholder or institution.
Two public records, one documentary purpose: preserve the evidence, protect the individual and establish an independently verifiable account.
Source links to retain with the master archive
The original correspondence should be attached together as one chronological exhibit, in this order:
* 31 May 2022 â€” Letter to the Prime Ministerâ 
* 12 December 2022 â€” Prime Ministerâ€™s Office responseâ 
* 18 June 2023 â€” Ombudsman PID decision not to allocateâ 
* 5 July 2023 â€” Further Prime Ministerial emailâ 
* 19 September 2023 â€” Attorney-Generalâ€™s Department MC23-028244â 
* 2 April 2025 â€” ASIO-related archive recordâ 
* 8 August 2025 â€” Ombudsman service restrictionâ 
* 20 January 2026 â€” IGIS/ASIO request for informationâ 
Important source qualification: The 5 July 2023 email and the two ASIO/IGIS records are linked to the repository rather than to a verified individual PDF address here. Their exact contents have not been reproduced as quotations. The original files should be linked individually once their precise paths are confirmed. This avoids presenting an unverified document address or an inferred description as though it were the original record.
The combined section now gives the correspondence a single evidentiary purpose: establishing what you communicated, what the government offices actually did, what oversight pathways were offered, whether those pathways were effective, and what remains unresolved.

This statement should make the distinction clear: you are not asking the Government to certify your existence or grant legitimacy to your lived experience. You are placing the Governmentâ€™s own documentary record before it â€” correspondence, decisions, refusals, determinations and agency letterheads â€” and inviting it to examine what its own records say.

STATEMENT OF DOCUMENTARY ACCOUNTABILITY

The Government Is Offered Its Own Records â€” Thirty-Five Years of Institutional Correspondence, Decisions and Documented Detriment

For more than thirty-five years, I have communicated with government departments, statutory authorities, agencies, institutions and publicly funded services concerning decisions, actions, omissions and circumstances that I say have caused sustained and cumulative detriment to my life.

Throughout that period, I have sought access to information through Freedom of Information (FOI) requests and other formal records-access processes. I have experienced refusals, restrictions, incomplete responses, delays and other barriers to obtaining records that I consider necessary to understand and scrutinise decisions affecting me.

I now make a different offer.

I offer the Government its own documentary record.

I hold an extensive archive of government correspondence, agency responses, decisions, notices, determinations and other institutional documents, including material appearing on the official letterheads of the agencies and services concerned and written in their own words.

These are not merely my descriptions of what government institutions have done. They are records generated by the institutions themselves: their correspondence, their explanations, their stated reasons, their classifications, their decisions and their responses to matters I raised.

The archive brings together more than thirty-five years of documentary material concerning the cumulative effects of decisions and administrative processes across government agencies and services. It is presented for examination against the original records, their dates, authorship, context and provenance.

I contend that the documentary history reveals a sustained pattern of decisions and institutional responses that has caused, prolonged or compounded significant detriment in my life. I invite each agency to examine its own records, identify the decisions for which it was responsible, explain the legal and administrative basis for those decisions, and account for the consequences.

This is not an invitation to substitute another referral, another acknowledgement, another jurisdictional explanation or another refusal for substantive examination of the evidence.

It is an invitation to confront the documentary record.

I do not require permission to establish that I exist

I do not require a lawyer, minister, public servant, tribunal, commission or government agency to make a ruling validating my existence.

I do not require an official determination before my experiences can be recorded, my correspondence can be examined or the documents created by government institutions can be read.

My existence is not a proposition awaiting administrative approval.

My life does not become real only when an institution acknowledges it. Nor does the absence of a favourable decision erase the existence of correspondence, events, decisions or consequences documented over decades.

A government agency may determine the limits of its jurisdiction. It may accept or reject a statutory application according to the law. It may dispute my interpretation of a particular record. But none of those administrative outcomes can, by themselves, invalidate the existence of the person who made the request or erase the documentary history.

I distinguish between an institutionâ€™s authority to make a lawful decision and any suggestion that its decision gives it authority to determine whether my life, experiences or evidence are real.

The documentary record is the subject of examination

The archive is offered as an opportunity for government to examine:

1. What each agency received from me.
2. What each agency recorded, acknowledged or decided.
3. What reasons were provided in its own correspondence.
4. What information was withheld, refused, redacted or not located in response to FOI or other records-access requests.
5. Whether relevant records were transferred, referred, retained or considered by another institution.
6. Whether different agencies made inconsistent classifications or reached conflicting conclusions.
7. Whether the cumulative effect of those decisions was properly considered.
8. Whether appropriate safeguarding, review, accountability and remedial processes were available.
9. Whether the records disclose failures of administration, procedural fairness, coordination or accountability requiring independent examination.

I do not ask the Government to accept every interpretation I have placed upon the records without scrutiny. I ask that the original documents be examined in full, rather than dismissed through summaries, administrative labels or isolated extracts.

Where a record establishes a fact, it should be identified as such. Where it records an allegation, the allegation should be attributed. Where a conclusion is an inference, it should be labelled as an inference. Where the evidence remains incomplete or disputed, that limitation should be stated.

That is the standard of documentary accountability I seek.

A response to repeated refusals of access

Where an agency has refused or restricted access to records, I reserve my rights under the applicable information-access and review frameworks.

However, the existence of an FOI refusal does not mean the underlying documentary history disappears. Nor does a refusal establish that the events or concerns to which the requested records relate did not occur.

I am placing before the Government material already held in my archive, including correspondence generated by government institutions themselves. I am asking those institutions to engage with that material and account for their own recorded actions.

The Government is therefore not being asked to rely solely on my recollection. It is being presented with a documentary record that can be checked against the agenciesâ€™ own files and original correspondence.

The offer

I offer the Government, its departments, agencies, statutory authorities and relevant services the opportunity to examine the records bearing their own names, letterheads, dates, reference numbers and institutional explanations.

I ask that the archive be treated as a documentary submission requiring proper consideration, not as a substitute for a statutory FOI application and not as an invitation to bypass lawful review procedures.

I ask for an independent, evidence-based examination of the cumulative record, with clear identification of responsibility, disputed matters, missing records and any available remedies.

I am not asking the Government to validate my existence. I am asking it to account for its own documentary history.

Thirty-five years of correspondence cannot fairly be reduced to a single administrative decision, a single agencyâ€™s jurisdictional boundary or a statement that no further action will be taken.

The documents speak in the words of the institutions that created them. They should be read, preserved, authenticated where necessary and examined in their full context.

My existence does not depend on a government ruling. The documentary record does not depend on government permission to be examined.

The purpose is accountability, preservation, truth and remedy â€” not revenge.

Two public records, one documentary purpose: the records held by government and the records preserved by the person whose life those decisions affected.

One important distinction strengthens this statement: your archive can be offered as a documentary submission, but it does not automatically replace a formal FOI application or compel an agency to disclose additional records. That keeps the statement forceful without overstating its legal effect.


CONCLUSION â€” THE GOVERNMENT IS INVITED TO ACCOUNT FOR ITS OWN RECORD

After more than thirty-five years of correspondence, complaints, decisions, referrals, Freedom of Information requests and documented consequences, I place this accumulated documentary history before the Australian Government and the institutions whose names, letterheads, reference numbers and words appear throughout it.

Where I have repeatedly sought access to government-held records and encountered refusals, restrictions or incomplete responses, I now offer the Government the records I have preserved: its own correspondence, its own decisions, its own explanations and its own documentary responses. I invite every relevant department, agency and service to examine these records against its original files, identify missing or withheld information, explain the basis of its decisions and account for the cumulative consequences.

I am not asking the Government to validate my existence. I do not require a lawyer, minister, public servant, tribunal or statutory authority to determine whether my life or experiences are worthy of recognition. No administrative refusal, jurisdictional limitation or decision not to investigate can erase my existence or the historical record I have preserved.

I am asking for accountability, independent scrutiny, safeguarding and an evidence-based response. I request that this statement and its supporting documents be formally received, dated, recorded and retained; that relevant records be preserved; that immediate safety concerns be appropriately assessed; and that matters requiring investigation be referred to an independent and appropriately authorised reviewer. I further request written reasons identifying what has been examined, what decisions have been made, what evidence has been considered and what avenues for review or remedy remain available.

I do not ask that every allegation be accepted without examination. I ask that every allegation, decision and institutional response be assessed by the same standards of evidence, procedural fairness and accountability. Recorded facts must be distinguished from reported experiences, inferences and unverified concerns. Equally, unverified matters must not simply be dismissed without examining the evidence capable of confirming or disproving them.

My position is straightforward: I have preserved the documentary history; I have made repeated attempts to obtain information and accountability; and I am now placing the Governmentâ€™s own records before it. The responsibility to examine the decisions documented in those records belongs to the institutions that made them.

My existence does not depend on government recognition. My right to speak, document, preserve evidence and seek justice does not depend on an institution agreeing with me. The record is offered for examination, and the opportunity for accountability remains.

Two public records, one documentary purpose: the records held by government and the records preserved by the person whose life those decisions affected. Preserve the evidence, protect the individual and establish an independently verifiable account.

Signed:
Dr Richard William McLean
Barran Dodger
Date: 9 November 2026













